pages.content:
This add-on generates a flyout sidebar which can help with mobile support.

![Show filters button example](https://facetwp.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/08/show-filters-button.png)To see it in action, browse to our [Cars demo](/demo/cars/) then shrink your browser width until you see the purple “Show filters” button.

![FacetWP Flyout menu add-on](https://facetwp.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/08/facetwp-flyout-menu.png)

By default, the flyout shows **all facets** that are on the page itself (except [Pager facets](/help-center/facets/facet-types/pager/), [Map facets](/help-center/facets/facet-types/map/) and [User Selections](/help-center/facets/facet-types/user-selections/)), in the same order as they are on the page, including multiple instances of the same facet.

If you want to limit the facets shown in the flyout, or change their order, see [the example below](#customize-the-facets-and-facet-order). User Selections (and other content or extra elements) can be added to the flyout by adding them to the flyout’s template, as demonstrated in the [example below](#add-user-selections-to-the-flyout).

## Usage

First, **download and install the add-on plugin**, and activate it.

Then, add a button or link with the CSS class 
```
facetwp-flyout-open
```

 to trigger the flyout:

### Add a button or link to open the flyout

Button example:

```
<button class="facetwp-flyout-open">Show filters</button>
```

Link example:

```
<a href="javascript:;" class="facetwp-flyout-open">Show filters</a>
```

#### Translate the button text

If you need to translate the button text, you’ll have to add the button with PHP:

```
How to use custom PHP code?PHP code can be added to your (child) theme's functions.php file. Alternatively, you can use the Custom Hooks add-on, or a code snippets plugin. More info echo '<button class="facetwp-flyout-open">'. facetwp_i18n( __( 'Show filters', 'fwp-front' ) ) . '</button>';
```

This makes the button text a translatable string that will appear in e.g. [WPML string translations](https://wpml.org/documentation/getting-started-guide/string-translation/) or any other translation plugin. The string can also be translated with WP’s [gettext filter](https://developer.wordpress.org/reference/hooks/gettext/) (see [this example](/help-center/facets/facet-types/autocomplete/#translate-the-ui-texts)). The button text can now also be translated [with the facetwp_i18n hook](/help-center/developers/hooks/advanced-hooks/facetwp_i18n/).

### Hide the flyout button or link on desktop widths

If you want to hide the button/link for desktop users, add the following to your 
```
style.css
```

. This hides the flyout button/link on window/device widths above 
```
779px
```

 (adjust this breakpoint as needed):

```
How to use custom CSS?CSS code can be placed in your (child) theme's style.css file. Alternatively, you can add it manually between <style> tags in the <head> section, in your (child) theme's header.php file. You can also load it with a hook in your (child) theme's functions.php file, or in the Custom Hooks add-on. To load the code only on pages with facets, use the facetwp_scripts hook. To load it on all pages, use wp_head or wp_footer.  Or you can use a code snippets plugin. More info@media (min-width: 780px) {
  .facetwp-flyout-open {
    display: none;
  }
}
```

You could also hide the facets on mobile window/device widths **below** the breakpoint width set in [line 1](#hide-flyout-button-desktop-L1), as [shown below](#hide-the-facets-on-mobile-widths).

### Hide the facets on mobile widths

If you want to hide the “desktop” facets on mobile widths, so they only appear in the flyout, you can add the following CSS.

The rule hides every facet (including the [User Selection facet](/help-center/facets/facet-types/user-selections/)) on the page (but not those appearing in the flyout), on mobile window/device widths **below** 
```
780px
```

 (adjust this breakpoint as needed).

Make sure the [flyout button is visible](#hide-the-flyout-button-or-link-on-desktop-widths) on mobile widths where the desktop facets are hidden.

```
How to use custom CSS?CSS code can be placed in your (child) theme's style.css file. Alternatively, you can add it manually between <style> tags in the <head> section, in your (child) theme's header.php file. You can also load it with a hook in your (child) theme's functions.php file, or in the Custom Hooks add-on. To load the code only on pages with facets, use the facetwp_scripts hook. To load it on all pages, use wp_head or wp_footer.  Or you can use a code snippets plugin. More info@media (max-width: 779px) {
  :not(.flyout-item) > .facetwp-facet,
  .facetwp-selections {
    display: none;
  }
}
```

Instead of targeting individual facet containers, you could also just hide a container element that your desktop facets are wrapped in.

Note that the facets need to be in the page’s HTML source to be able to be detected by the flyout’s JavaScript, so don’t try to remove them in any other way.

### Add a flyout button with an Elementor FacetWP Button widget

![Add a flyout button with an Elementor add-on FacetWP Button widget.](https://facetwp.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/facetwp-elementor-addon-button-widget-flyout.png)Add a flyout button with an Elementor add-on FacetWP Button widget.
If you are using Elementor, with [the Elementor add-on](/help-center/using-facetwp-with/elementor/) installed, you can [use the FacetWP Button widget](/help-center/using-facetwp-with/elementor/#the-facetwp-button-widget) to place a flyout button on your page.

Just add the FacetWP Button widget, and select “Flyout button” as the button type.

### Add a flyout button with a Beaver Builder FacetWP Button module

![Add a flyout button with a Beaver Builder FacetWP Button module.](https://facetwp.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/bb-facetwp-button-module-flyout.png)Add a flyout button with a Beaver Builder FacetWP Button module.
If you are using Beaver Builder, with [the Beaver Builder add-on](/help-center/using-facetwp-with/beaver-builder/) installed, you can [use the FacetWP Button module](/help-center/using-facetwp-with/beaver-builder/#the-facetwp-button-module) to place a flyout button on your page.

Just add the FacetWP Button module, and select “Flyout button” as the button type.

## Facet headings / labels

The Mobile Flyout automatically adds each facet’s [label](/help-center/facets/#the-facet-label) as 
```
<h3>
```

 heading above it.

If you want to change the HTML of these headings, for example change the 
```
<h3>
```

 to 
```
<h2>
```

, see [the example below](#modify-each-facets-html).

### Hide the facet headings / labels

If you want to hide all facet headings in the flyout, add the following into your (child) theme’s 
```
style.css
```

:

```
How to use custom CSS?CSS code can be placed in your (child) theme's style.css file. Alternatively, you can add it manually between <style> tags in the <head> section, in your (child) theme's header.php file. You can also load it with a hook in your (child) theme's functions.php file, or in the Custom Hooks add-on. To load the code only on pages with facets, use the facetwp_scripts hook. To load it on all pages, use wp_head or wp_footer.  Or you can use a code snippets plugin. More info.flyout-row h3 {
  display: none;
}
```

To hide only specific facet headings, you can use the following CSS instead:

```
How to use custom CSS?CSS code can be placed in your (child) theme's style.css file. Alternatively, you can add it manually between <style> tags in the <head> section, in your (child) theme's header.php file. You can also load it with a hook in your (child) theme's functions.php file, or in the Custom Hooks add-on. To load the code only on pages with facets, use the facetwp_scripts hook. To load it on all pages, use wp_head or wp_footer.  Or you can use a code snippets plugin. More info/* Replace 'myfacetname' with the name of your facet */
.flyout-row.name-myfacetname h3 {
  display: none;
}
```

And if you want to hide one or more of these headings in specific situations only, you can use the [Conditional Logic add-on](/help-center/add-on-features-and-extras/conditional-logic/) with a rule that uses a [custom selector](/help-center/add-on-features-and-extras/conditional-logic/#conditionally-hide-facet-labels-in-the-mobile-flyout).

### Remove or change the facet headings / labels

If you want to remove the facet headings from the flyout code entirely, you can use the following code:

```
How to use custom PHP code?PHP code can be added to your (child) theme's functions.php file. Alternatively, you can use the Custom Hooks add-on, or a code snippets plugin. More infoadd_action( 'facetwp_scripts', function() {
  ?>
  <script>
    (function($) {
      $(function() {
        if ('object' != typeof FWP) return;

        /* Modify each facet's wrapper HTML */
        FWP.hooks.addFilter('facetwp/flyout/facet_html', function(facet_html) {
          return facet_html.replace('<h3>{label}</h3>', ''); // Sets the heading output to an empty string
        });
      });
    })(fUtil);
  </script>
  <?php
}, 100 );
```

The same snippet can be changed slightly to [change the headings’ HTML tag](#modify-each-facets-html).

### Translate the facet headings / labels

The facet labels/headings can be translated with the [facetwp_i18n hook](/help-center/developers/hooks/advanced-hooks/facetwp_i18n/).

## JavaScript functions

The following functions are available to open and close the flyout:

```
How to use custom JavaScript code?JavaScript code can be placed in your (child) theme's main JavaScript file. Alternatively, you can add it manually between <script> tags in the <head> section of your (child) theme's header.php file. You can also load it with a hook in your (child) theme's functions.php file, or in the Custom Hooks add-on. To load the code only on pages with facets, use the facetwp_scripts hook. To load it on all pages, use wp_head or wp_footer. Or you can use a code snippets plugin. More infoFWP.flyout.open(); // open the flyout
FWP.flyout.close(); // close it
```

## JavaScript hooks

The following hooks are available to customize the flyout’s content and behavior:

### Customize the facets and facet order in the flyout

By default, the flyout shows **all** facets that are on the page itself (except [Pager](/help-center/facets/facet-types/pager/) and [Map](/help-center/facets/facet-types/map/) facets), in the same order.

If you want to **limit the facets shown in the flyout, or change their order**, add the following code to your (child) theme’s functions.php.

Add the desired (or all) facet names in the [array in line 11](#js-hooks-facets-L11), in the order you want them to appear in the flyout. If desired, you can [Pager facets](/help-center/facets/facet-types/pager/), which are not in the flyout by default.

The filter in [lines 14-16](#js-hooks-facets-L14-L16) makes sure that the facet names in the array actually exist on the page. In previous versions of this snippet (without this check), you’d get the error 
```
$this is undefined
```

 in the browser Console if you included facets that were not on the page, and the flyout would malfunction. If you see this error, replace your existing snippet with the one below.

If you have one or more [Reset facets](/help-center/facets/facet-types/reset/) on your page, and you want them to appear in the flyout, adding them to the [array in line 11](#js-hooks-facets-L11) will not work, as they are not in the 
```
FWP.facets
```

 object that this array is checked against. If you want one or more of them in the flyout, add its/their name(s) to the array in [lines 20](#js-hooks-facets-L20). If you don’t have Reset facets or don’t want them in the flyout, make sure to **remove** [lines 20 and 21](#js-hooks-facets-L20-L21) to prevent errors.

If you have more than one page with facets, and you want a different set of facets in the flyout of different pages/templates, wrap the script with a [Conditional Tag](https://codex.wordpress.org/Conditional_Tags), as shown in line [line 2](#js-hooks-facets-L2) (and the closing bracket on [line 29](#js-hooks-facets-L29)), and replicate the code for each facet page where you want to change the set (and/or order) of facets. If you want to have the same set of facets on all pages, just remove the Conditional Tag.

```
How to use custom PHP code?PHP code can be added to your (child) theme's functions.php file. Alternatively, you can use the Custom Hooks add-on, or a code snippets plugin. More infoadd_action( 'facetwp_scripts', function() {
  if( is_page( 123 ) ) { // Optional: use any Conditional Tag: https://codex.wordpress.org/Conditional_Tags to let this script run only on the desired page
    ?>
    <script>
      (function($) {
